      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(proxy-capture): time out stalled CONNECT upstreams",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe debug proxy's HTTP `CONNECT` path opened its upstream TCP socket without a deadline. A blackholed or stalled target could therefore leave the client request and capture session pending indefinitely.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nGive upstream CONNECT establishment a 30-second opening deadline. On timeout, the proxy records a local capture error, explicitly destroys the upstream socket, and returns a complete `504 Gateway Timeout` response to the client.\n\nNode's socket timeout only emits an event; it does not close the socket, so explicit destruction is required. Once the upstream emits `connect`, the implementation disables the timer and removes the opening-timeout listener so long-lived CONNECT tunnels do not inherit an idle timeout.\n\n## User Impact\n\nDebug proxy clients now receive a bounded, actionable 504 response when a CONNECT target never establishes. Successfully connected tunnels remain long-lived exactly as before.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Node 24.15.0: `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/proxy-capture/proxy-server.test.ts` (5/5 tests passed)\n- `oxfmt --check` on both changed files\n- `oxlint` on both changed files\n- `git diff origin/main...HEAD --check`\n- Controlled Node socket proof: the timeout event fired at about 121ms and `destroyedBeforeHandler` was false, confirming that explicit `destroy()` is necessary\n- Node 24.15.0 `net` source was checked directly for pre-connect timeout setup, event-only timeout behavior, timer removal, and destroyed-socket connect handling\n- Independent adversarial review: clean, best fix for this boundary, estimated 86% merge probability\n\nProof gap: validation used deterministic socket tests and a controlled runtime probe rather than a real external blackholed host or a long-duration live CONNECT tunnel.\n",
